
Neither "calls" anything automatically — your code (otc_workflow.py) decides the order, and it's actually both, at different points in the same request:

1. Blob (read, via mount)         <- App Service code opens the file
2. Azure AI Search (WRITE)        <- App Service code chunks+embeds+uploads
       (this is the ingestion step — happens FIRST, before any LLM call)
3. Azure AI Search (READ)         <- App Service code queries for relevant chunks
4. Foundry (chat completion)      <- App Service code sends those chunks + a
                                      prompt, gets back extracted/drafted text
5. repeat steps 3-4 for every extraction/GDG/drafting step

So within one request: Search gets called twice (once to index, many times to retrieve), Foundry gets called once per extraction/analysis/drafting step. Blob is read once up front. There's no single "it calls Foundry or Search" moment — it's a sequence your code drives.

Question 2 — does Foundry call Search itself, or is it all App Service?

100% App Service. This is the important part to get right: Azure AI Foundry does have a real, separate feature — "Agent Service" with a built-in Azure AI Search tool — where you hand the agent a Search index and the hosted agent decides on its own, mid-conversation, when to call Search (like function-calling/tool-use). This codebase does not use that feature at all. That option was explicitly looked at and rejected earlier in this project (same reasoning as rejecting the SharePoint grounding tool — see option-otc memory).

Instead, what's actually happening is a classic hand-rolled RAG pattern:

App Service code:
  1. asks Search: "give me chunks matching this query"   (plain SDK call)
  2. takes the returned TEXT
  3. pastes that text into a prompt string:
       "Here is some evidence: <chunks>. Extract these fields: <...>"
  4. sends that whole prompt to Foundry's chat endpoint    (plain SDK call)
  5. Foundry has no idea Search exists — it just sees one big text
     prompt and returns completion text

Foundry in this design is a dumb text-in/text-out endpoint — it never sees Search, never decides to query it, never knows it's being handed RAG-retrieved context versus hand-typed text. The App Service's Python code is the only thing aware of both services, and it's the only thing that connects them — by literally string-concatenating Search's output into Foundry's input.

