❯ my another app service i need to confogure search seracive manageed identity right?

Yes — exactly right. Here's the concrete steps for that App Service:

1. Enable Managed Identity on the App Service
Portal → your App Service → Identity → System assigned → On → Save. (User-assigned works too if you prefer a shared identity across resources, but system-assigned is simpler for a single App Service.)

2. Assign it roles on the Azure AI Search resource
Portal → the Search service → Access control (IAM) → Add role assignment:
- Search Service Contributor — needed for index_chunks()'s create_index()/delete_index() calls
- Search Index Data Contributor — needed for upload_documents() and the retrieval queries
- Assign both to: Managed identity → select your App Service's identity

3. Assign it a role on the Azure OpenAI/Foundry resource too (don't forget this one — it's separate from Search)
Portal → that Cognitive Services/AI Foundry resource → Access control (IAM) → Add role assignment → Cognitive Services OpenAI User → same App Service identity. Without this, embeddings and the chat/completion calls both fail even if Search is perfectly configured.

4. App settings — leave the key out
Set AI_SEARCH_ENDPOINT to the Search resource's URL, and don't set AI_SEARCH_API_KEY at all — its absence is what makes the code take the DefaultAzureCredential() branch instead of the key branch. Same pattern for OTC_AOAI_ENDPOINT (no key setting exists for that one in what we've seen — it's AAD-only already).

5. No code/SDK config needed beyond that
DefaultAzureCredential running inside App Service automatically discovers and uses whichever managed identity you enabled in step 1 — Azure injects the identity endpoint/token plumbing into the App Service environment for you. Nothing to configure on the code side once the identity exists and has the roles.
